From a10723937182cb57a20e5022bd3f9ca46f6a7b6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tm <tm@x>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:37:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 33/36] Compiler: internal defines lowered once, before the
 compilation (lower-define)

From Julia's femtolisp (0c4abb27f3): lower-define rewrites the internal
defines of a whole form, as lambdas applied to the unspecified values,
before compile-f- runs, instead of compile-f- doing it for each lambda
with a marker in the name position (%defines-processed%, the fixed
symbol of 0009). compile, compile-thunk and compile-f- are plain
functions, and the bodies seen by compile-f- are lowered (the closure
analysis of the next patches needs that).

TeXmacs: with *keep-source* (0011), a lowered lambda keeps the source of
the lambda it comes from, (lambda args body (%source . source) . name),
which compile-f- adds as the last constant: procedure-source still gives
the lambda as it was expanded, internal defines included, and the
lambdas made for the internal defines and the thunks of compile-thunk
have none, as before. The form given by compile-unknown-call (0010) is
lowered too; get-defined-vars keeps define-head-name (0008). The default
values of the optional and keyword arguments are lowered as well (in
Julia's femtolisp, a lambda there kept its body of several forms, of
which compile-f- compiled the first one only).

mkboot0.lsp takes --compiler compiler.lsp, which loads a new compiler
before compiling the files (the cross step when the compiler or the
bytecode changes: the old flisp makes the boot image of the new one),
and mkboot1.lsp compiles all the forms of compiler.lsp before running
them, so that the compiler does not change while it compiles itself.
Boot image regenerated:
  old/flisp mkboot0.lsp --compiler compiler.lsp system.lsp compiler.lsp
  then ./flisp mkboot1.lsp until flisp.boot no longer changes.
